# Copperplate: the seven basic strokes, and letters built from them.
#
# Copperplate is written with a pointed pen whose tines spread under
# pressure, so a stroke swells and thins along its length. chi has no
# pressure — but a node's width is its own, so pressure is just which nib a
# node carries, and the fitter draws the swell between them. The pressure
# profile is sampled at nodes exactly as an outline is sampled at points.
#
# The nib is round, and that is not a simplification. A pointed pen's slit
# runs along the direction of travel, so the tines open across it and the
# stroke thickens perpendicular to travel — which is what a circular pen
# does at every angle. Three of them here; pressure is the choice between
# them.
#
# Nothing is authored twice. Each stroke declares its pen path — the
# gesture, three or four points — and then places pressure *along* it:
#
#     e = press(spine, 2.45, full);
#
# reads as "at 2.45 of the way along, full weight". Both the position and
# the direction come from the path, so the banks are true offsets of the
# curve that renders rather than of angles guessed alongside it. Sampling
# density is then free, which matters: an arch shoulder turns 125° in one
# segment, and without nodes inside the turn the inner bank has nothing to
# trace and folds over itself.
#
# A letter is one contour per pen stroke, all counterclockwise, overlapping
# where the strokes meet. The nonzero rule unions them — which is also how
# the letter is written, one stroke at a time.
#
# Where it runs out
#   * Pressure is a step function over three nibs. A real swell is
#     continuous, and its shape — where the stroke reaches full weight, how
#     quickly it lets go — is most of what distinguishes one hand from
#     another. Here that shape is only whatever the fitter does between two
#     samples, and the only control is more samples.
#   * Pressure and curvature interact, and nothing says so. Widening the pen
#     where the path turns tightest folds the inner bank over itself and
#     bites a wedge out of the stroke; an arch shoulder is exactly such a
#     place. The rule is that the pen's radius must stay under the radius of
#     curvature, which copperplate obeys for free — you press on the
#     straight pull and release into the turns — but here it is a trap with
#     no diagnostic, and the symptom is a shape that looks gnawed. Every
#     `hairline` on a shoulder below is load-bearing.
#   * Every stroke spells out the same cap-and-return idiom, because a def
#     cannot take a variable number of nodes and a path cannot be spliced
#     into another path. Two thirds of each stroke is that boilerplate.
#   * A stroke is a macro, not geometry: 'm' re-runs an overturn twice
#     rather than placing one twice.
#   * Nothing carries the notion of pressure *rising*. The compound stroke
#     swells twice and both swells are placed by hand.
#   * Seven strokes are not an alphabet. The five letters below are the
#     combinations the reference spells out, and they cost nothing beyond
#     placing strokes — but the rest of the lowercase needs the three more
#     strokes it also names: the dot, the flat top, and the small underturn.
#     The combinatorial promise is real; the count is ten, not seven.

params {
  slant    = 55 in 40..70;    # the shade axis, degrees from the baseline
  shade    = 46 in 12..90;    # full-pressure thickness
  contrast = 11 in 3..24;     # shade / hairline
  xheight  = 250 in 150..320;
}

metrics {
  upm  = 1000;
  x    = xheight;
  asc  = 2.6 xheight;         # engrosser's proportions are extreme
  desc = -1.3 xheight;
  cap  = 2 xheight;
}

hair  = shade / contrast;
half  = 0.48 (shade + hair);
shear = 1 / tan(slant);       # rightward push per unit of height
aw    = 0.66 x;               # arch width: the run of one letter part
sb    = 0.18 x;               # sidebearing

# The three pressures.
pen hairline { width = hair;  height = hair; }
pen medium   { width = half;  height = half; }
pen full     { width = shade; height = shade; }

axis_up   = slant;            # travelling up the shade axis
axis_down = slant + 180;      # and down it

# A point `run` along the baseline and `rise` above it, pushed right by the
# slant, so that a fixed run traces the shade axis.
def at(run, rise) -> p { p = (run + shear * rise, rise); }

# A pressure sample: a node on the pen path at segment time `t`, carrying
# `nib`. Position and direction both come from the path.
def press(path, t, nib) -> n {
  node n = point(path, t) dir direction(path, t) pen nib;
}

# ---- The seven strokes -----------------------------------------------------

# (1) The entrance stroke: hairline throughout, swinging up from the
# baseline to meet the letter. No pressure at all.
def entrance(run) -> (outline, spine) {
  spine = (run, 0.03 x) {30} .. (run + 0.45 aw, 0.24 x)
       .. at(run + 0.55 aw, 0.52 x) {axis_up};
  a = press(spine, 0,   hairline);
  b = press(spine, 1,   hairline);
  c = press(spine, 1.6, hairline);
  d = press(spine, 2,   hairline);

  outline =
      {a.dir} a.right .. b.right .. c.right .. d.right {d.dir}
   .. {d.dir + 90} d.tip {d.dir + 90}
   .. {d.dir + 180} d.left .. c.left .. b.left .. a.left {a.dir + 180}
   .. {a.dir + 270} a.tail {a.dir + 270}
   .. cycle;
}

# (2) The oval: a closed pen path, shaded down its left flank and hairline
# up its right. Travelling counterclockwise, the right bank is the outer
# contour and the left bank — taken in reverse — is the counter.
def oval(run) -> (outer, counter, spine) {
  cu = run + 0.5 aw;
  ru = 0.38 aw;
  spine = at(cu, x) .. at(cu - ru, 0.5 x) .. at(cu, 0) .. at(cu + ru, 0.5 x)
       .. cycle;
  a = press(spine, 0,   medium);      # top
  b = press(spine, 0.5, full);
  c = press(spine, 1,   full);        # left flank: the shade
  d = press(spine, 1.5, medium);
  e = press(spine, 2,   hairline);    # bottom
  f = press(spine, 2.5, hairline);
  g = press(spine, 3,   hairline);    # right flank
  h = press(spine, 3.5, hairline);

  outer = a.right .. b.right .. c.right .. d.right .. e.right .. f.right
       .. g.right .. h.right .. cycle;
  counter = a.left .. h.left .. g.left .. f.left .. e.left .. d.left
       .. c.left .. b.left .. cycle;
}

# (3) The underturn: down the shade axis under pressure, round the baseline,
# away as a hairline. The turn is where the pen has already let go.
def underturn(run) -> (outline, spine) {
  spine = at(run, x) {axis_down} .. (run + 0.30 x, 0.14 x)
       .. {right} (run + 0.5 aw, 0) .. at(run + aw, 0.52 x) {axis_up};
  a = press(spine, 0,    medium);
  b = press(spine, 0.35, full);
  c = press(spine, 0.7,  full);
  d = press(spine, 1,    medium);
  e = press(spine, 1.5,  hairline);
  f = press(spine, 2,    hairline);
  g = press(spine, 3,    hairline);

  outline =
      {a.dir} a.right .. b.right .. c.right .. d.right .. e.right .. f.right
   .. g.right {g.dir}
   .. {g.dir + 90} g.tip {g.dir + 90}
   .. {g.dir + 180} g.left .. f.left .. e.left .. d.left .. c.left .. b.left
   .. a.left {a.dir + 180}
   .. {a.dir + 270} a.tail {a.dir + 270}
   .. cycle;
}

# (4) The overturn: hairline up, over the shoulder, then down under
# pressure. The mirror of the underturn, pressure and all.
def overturn(run) -> (outline, spine) {
  spine = (run, 0.03 x) {50} .. at(run + 0.1 aw, 0.6 x)
       .. {right} at(run + aw, x) .. at(run + aw, 0.03 x) {axis_down};
  a = press(spine, 0,    hairline);
  b = press(spine, 1,    hairline);
  c = press(spine, 2,    hairline);   # the shoulder stays light
  d = press(spine, 2.4,  medium);
  e = press(spine, 2.65, full);
  f = press(spine, 2.85, full);
  g = press(spine, 3,    medium);

  outline =
      {a.dir} a.right .. b.right .. c.right .. d.right .. e.right .. f.right
   .. g.right {g.dir}
   .. {g.dir + 90} g.tip {g.dir + 90}
   .. {g.dir + 180} g.left .. f.left .. e.left .. d.left .. c.left .. b.left
   .. a.left {a.dir + 180}
   .. {a.dir + 270} a.tail {a.dir + 270}
   .. cycle;
}

# (5) The compound: an overturn and an underturn in one pen stroke — up,
# over, down under pressure, round the bottom, away. Two pressure events on
# one path, which is where the want of a dynamic nib shows most.
def compound(run) -> (outline, spine) {
  spine = (run, 0.03 x) {50} .. at(run + 0.1 aw, 0.6 x)
       .. {right} at(run + aw, x) .. (run + aw + 0.30 x, 0.14 x)
       .. {right} (run + 1.5 aw, 0) .. at(run + 2 aw, 0.52 x) {axis_up};
  a = press(spine, 0,    hairline);
  b = press(spine, 1,    hairline);
  c = press(spine, 2,    hairline);   # the shoulder stays light
  d = press(spine, 2.45, medium);
  e = press(spine, 2.75, full);
  f = press(spine, 3,    medium);
  g = press(spine, 3.5,  hairline);
  h = press(spine, 4,    hairline);
  i = press(spine, 5,    hairline);

  outline =
      {a.dir} a.right .. b.right .. c.right .. d.right .. e.right .. f.right
   .. g.right .. h.right .. i.right {i.dir}
   .. {i.dir + 90} i.tip {i.dir + 90}
   .. {i.dir + 180} i.left .. h.left .. g.left .. f.left .. e.left .. d.left
   .. c.left .. b.left .. a.left {a.dir + 180}
   .. {a.dir + 270} a.tail {a.dir + 270}
   .. cycle;
}

# (6) The ascender: a hairline loop over the top, then the shaded stem down
# to the baseline, crossing the loop on the way.
def ascender(run) -> (outline, spine) {
  lu = run + 0.34 aw;
  spine = at(lu, 0.5 asc) {72} .. at(lu + 0.3 aw, 0.84 asc)
       .. {170} at(lu + 0.02 aw, asc) .. at(lu - 0.26 aw, 0.8 asc)
       .. at(run, 0.42 asc) .. at(run, 0.03 x) {axis_down};
  a = press(spine, 0,   hairline);
  b = press(spine, 1,   hairline);
  c = press(spine, 2,   hairline);
  d = press(spine, 2.6, hairline);
  e = press(spine, 3,   medium);
  f = press(spine, 3.6, full);
  g = press(spine, 4,   full);
  h = press(spine, 5,   medium);

  outline =
      {a.dir} a.right .. b.right .. c.right .. d.right .. e.right .. f.right
   .. g.right .. h.right {h.dir}
   .. {h.dir + 90} h.tip {h.dir + 90}
   .. {h.dir + 180} h.left .. g.left .. f.left .. e.left .. d.left .. c.left
   .. b.left .. a.left {a.dir + 180}
   .. {a.dir + 270} a.tail {a.dir + 270}
   .. cycle;
}

# (7) The descender: the shaded stem down through the baseline, then the
# loop swinging out below and back up as a hairline, crossing the stem.
def descender(run) -> (outline, spine) {
  spine = at(run, x) {axis_down} .. at(run, 0.42 desc)
       .. {185} at(run - 0.34 aw, desc) .. at(run - 0.74 aw, 0.42 desc)
       .. (run + 0.34 aw, 0.08 x) {28};
  a = press(spine, 0,    medium);
  b = press(spine, 0.35, full);
  c = press(spine, 0.75, full);
  d = press(spine, 1,    medium);
  e = press(spine, 1.5,  hairline);
  f = press(spine, 2,    hairline);
  g = press(spine, 3,    hairline);
  h = press(spine, 4,    hairline);

  outline =
      {a.dir} a.right .. b.right .. c.right .. d.right .. e.right .. f.right
   .. g.right .. h.right {h.dir}
   .. {h.dir + 90} h.tip {h.dir + 90}
   .. {h.dir + 180} h.left .. g.left .. f.left .. e.left .. d.left .. c.left
   .. b.left .. a.left {a.dir + 180}
   .. {a.dir + 270} a.tail {a.dir + 270}
   .. cycle;
}

# ---- The strokes as glyphs -------------------------------------------------
#
# Each shows its own pen path as a guide, so --debug reads as the gesture
# plus the pressure its nodes carry.

glyph entrance {
  advance = aw + shear * x + 2 sb;
  (o, k) = entrance(sb);
  contour s = o;
  guide pen_path = k;
}

glyph oval {
  advance = aw + shear * x + 2 sb;
  (body, hole, k) = oval(sb);
  contour outer = body;
  contour inner = hole;
  guide pen_path = k;
}

glyph underturn {
  advance = aw + shear * x + 2 sb;
  (o, k) = underturn(sb);
  contour s = o;
  guide pen_path = k;
}

glyph overturn {
  advance = aw + shear * x + 2 sb;
  (o, k) = overturn(sb);
  contour s = o;
  guide pen_path = k;
}

glyph compound {
  advance = 2 aw + shear * x + 2 sb;
  (o, k) = compound(sb);
  contour s = o;
  guide pen_path = k;
}

glyph ascender {
  advance = aw + shear * asc + 2 sb;
  (o, k) = ascender(sb);
  contour s = o;
  guide pen_path = k;
}

glyph descender {
  advance = aw + shear * x + 4 sb;
  (o, k) = descender(sb + 0.8 aw);
  contour s = o;
  guide pen_path = k;
}

# ---- Letters, as the strokes that write them ------------------------------

# (6) ascender + (5) compound
glyph h : U+0068 {
  advance = 2 aw + shear * x + 2 sb;
  (stem, stem_path) = ascender(sb);
  (arch, arch_path) = compound(sb + 0.05 aw);
  contour s1 = stem;
  contour s2 = arch;
  guide k1 = stem_path;
  guide k2 = arch_path;
}

# (5) compound + (3) underturn. No stroke (1): the compound opens with an
# upstroke of its own, which is the entrance.
glyph u : U+0075 {
  advance = 3 aw + shear * x + 2 sb;
  (first, first_path) = compound(sb);
  (last, last_path)   = underturn(sb + 2 aw);
  contour s1 = first;
  contour s2 = last;
  guide k1 = first_path;
  guide k2 = last_path;
}

# (4) overturn + (4) overturn + (5) compound
glyph m : U+006D {
  advance = 4 aw + shear * x + 2 sb;
  (first, first_path)   = overturn(sb);
  (second, second_path) = overturn(sb + aw);
  (last, last_path)     = compound(sb + 2 aw);
  contour s1 = first;
  contour s2 = second;
  contour s3 = last;
  guide k1 = first_path;
  guide k2 = second_path;
  guide k3 = last_path;
}

# (4) overturn + (5) compound — the same two strokes 'm' ends with.
glyph n : U+006E {
  advance = 3 aw + shear * x + 2 sb;
  (first, first_path) = overturn(sb);
  (last, last_path)   = compound(sb + aw);
  contour s1 = first;
  contour s2 = last;
  guide k1 = first_path;
  guide k2 = last_path;
}

# (1) entrance + (2) oval + (7) descender. The oval opens on a downstroke,
# so this is a letter that does need the entrance stroke.
glyph g : U+0067 {
  advance = 2 aw + shear * x + 2 sb;
  (lead, lead_path)       = entrance(sb - 0.5 aw);
  (body, hole, body_path) = oval(sb);
  (tail, tail_path)       = descender(sb + 0.95 aw);
  contour s1 = lead;
  contour s2 = body;
  contour s3 = hole;
  contour s4 = tail;
  guide k1 = body_path;
  guide k2 = tail_path;
}
